    "breadcrumb": "CODAC",
    "content": "The center’s work is organized into five intertwined thrusts, unified by their use of category theory to provide a compositional, interoperable foundation. Each task has a responsible lead, but all investigators collaborate across thrusts. Sharing mathematical results and software implementations between them is what makes the center more than five parallel projects.\nComputational sheaf theory cuts across all five: it is developed in Thrust 1 and used to operationalize the rest.\nComputational Sheaf Theory Thrust 1: abstractions and algorithms in computational sheaf theory, general enough to span optimization, dynamics, and control, concrete enough to implement.\nLearning and Control for Open Systems Thrust 2: composite energy-driven systems, spanning learning systems that optimize objectives and dynamical systems that obey classical mechanics.\nCompositional Optimization Algorithms Thrust 3: cellular sheaves and homological programs for decentralized optimization under impaired, asynchronous communication.\nContext-Aware Decisions Under Uncertainty Thrust 4: Moore machine formulations of POMDPs and stochastic games, and policies robust to several kinds of uncertainty at once.\nScalable Implementation and Deployment Thrust 5: a user-facing platform and domain-specific languages that put the center’s methods in the hands of practicing engineers.",
    "description": "The five intertwined research thrusts of the DIGICAMS center.",
    "tags": [],
    "title": "Research thrusts",
    "uri": "/thrusts/index.html"
