    "breadcrumb": "CODAC",
    "content": "The center produces large-scale computational tools for specifying and solving complex engineering problems, together with the user-facing platform that delivers them. All software is open source and immediately available to the government to support future AFRL science and technology efforts.\nDevelopment happens in the open, across three homes:\nCODAC-Centers on GitHub: the center’s own repositories. AlgebraicJulia: the applied category theory ecosystem in Julia that much of the center’s computational work builds on and contributes to. CatColab: the collaborative environment for formal, interoperable, conceptual modeling developed with the Topos Institute. Work under Thrust 5 develops domain-specific languages for the composition paradigms and system specifications produced in the other thrusts.\nProjects Cellular Sheaf Explorer Computes global sections of a cellular sheaf over a graph, in the browser. Six worked examples, a source editor for writing your own sheaf, and the spectrum and energy readouts behind every claim.\nWhat it does Multi-Domain Tracking The tracking scenario as a standalone widget, embedded and ready to drag. Pin the ships and four degrees of freedom survive, one altitude per target; pin the targets and none do.\nRun it",
