    "breadcrumb": "CODAC",
    "content": "The center brings together control, optimization, cyber-physical systems, and applied category theory. That mix means there is no single background we expect: people arrive from dynamics and control, from optimization, from programming languages and category theory, and the work happens where those meet.\nWho we are looking for Graduate students interested in compositional methods for optimization, dynamics, and control. Postdoctoral researchers in applied category theory, sheaf theory, decentralized optimization, or assured autonomy. Undergraduate researchers who want to work on open-source scientific software. Working knowledge of category theory is not a prerequisite. A central aim of the center is to translate categorical results back into standard engineering formalisms, and that goal shapes how people are brought into the work.\nStudents and faculty at AFRL Students and faculty alike pursue summer fellowships with AFRL over the course of the project. These make the needs of the Air Force concrete and open the door for students to pursue Air Force careers.\nGetting in touch Write to James Fairbanks with a CV and a short note about what you want to work on. Prospective graduate students should also apply through the relevant departmental admissions process.",
    "description": "Opportunities for graduate students, postdocs, and undergraduate researchers at the DIGICAMS center.",
