    "breadcrumb": "",
    "content": "AFOSR Center of Excellence\nCompositional decision-making for modern systems Optimization, dynamics, and control are analyzed on separate mathematical foundations. We are building one foundation that spans them, so that the properties engineers prove about parts survive in the systems they are built into.\nThe research plan Who we are\nModern autonomous systems are complex enough that designing and operating them takes several disciplines of engineering at once. To reach a destination, a swarm of drones may formulate an optimization problem encoding its dynamical constraints, solve that problem to plan a trajectory, and then regulate to that trajectory with an onboard controller. Those three considerations are usually analyzed individually, on disparate mathematical foundations.\nThat separation hides problems. Each subsystem can be optimal in isolation while their composite is not, in ways that are hard to detect. Stable subsystems can compose into an unstable whole. Resources can sit persistently misallocated because no single analysis sees the whole allocation.\nDIGICAMS stands for Designing Integrated, Generic, and Interoperable Compositional Analyses of Modern Systems. It develops unified mathematical frameworks for the composition of decision-making processes across optimization, dynamical systems, and control. “Composition” here means modeling interconnections of systems along with the properties and behaviors those interconnections produce. The center was awarded by AFOSR on 19 May 2025.\nWhat makes this different Prior work has used category theory to model engineering problems and left the solutions in the language of category theory. We start from engineering problems, use category theory to model, generalize, and solve them, and then translate the solutions back into the engineering formalisms they came from, so that working knowledge of category theory is not a prerequisite for benefiting from the result.\nRead the approach Research thrusts The work is organized into five thrusts, each with its own lead.\nComputational Sheaf Theory Thrust 1: abstractions and algorithms in computational sheaf theory, general enough to span optimization, dynamics, and control, concrete enough to implement.\nLearning and Control for Open Systems Thrust 2: composite energy-driven systems, spanning learning systems that optimize objectives and dynamical systems that obey classical mechanics.\nCompositional Optimization Algorithms Thrust 3: cellular sheaves and homological programs for decentralized optimization under impaired, asynchronous communication.\nContext-Aware Decisions Under Uncertainty Thrust 4: Moore machine formulations of POMDPs and stochastic games, and policies robust to several kinds of uncertainty at once.\nScalable Implementation and Deployment Thrust 5: a user-facing platform and domain-specific languages that put the center’s methods in the hands of practicing engineers.\nLatest news 21 September 2026\nSix center papers accepted across CDC, ACC, ECC, IFAC and Asilomar Work from across the center appears at the Conference on Decision and Control, the American Control Conference, the European Control Conference, IFAC and Asilomar.\nMay 2026\nTyler Hanks and Cristian Nino complete their PhDs Two of the center’s students have finished: Tyler Hanks on coordination sheaves, and Cristian Nino on multi-agent tracking and control.\n19 May 2025\nAFOSR awards the DIGICAMS Center of Excellence A $3.5 million Center of Excellence award for Designing Integrated, Generic, and Interoperable Compositional Analyses of Modern Systems.\nAll news\nExplore the center Research The mathematical framework behind CODAC: categories of systems, functorial semantics, and sheaves as the common language for problems and solutions.\nPeople The people of the DIGICAMS center, spanning control, optimization, cyber-physical systems, and applied category theory.\nSoftware Software from the center: all of it open source, and immediately available to the government.\nJoin Opportunities for graduate students, postdocs, and undergraduate researchers at the DIGICAMS center.",
    "description": "CODAC is a University of Florida-led center developing compositional methods for decision-making across optimization, dynamical systems, and control, using applied category theory and computational sheaf theory.",
    "tags": [],
    "title": "CODAC",
    "uri": "/index.html"
